= Capability model =

Content architecture and extension architecture are separate concerns.

== Core knowledge capabilities ==
The core wiki benefits from:
* revisioned MediaWiki pages and native namespaces;
* categories and search;
* templates and Scribunto modules;
* citations and source handling;
* human editing interfaces;
* Semantic MediaWiki for typed relationships and semantic retrieval;
* APIs and export surfaces;
* identity and permission integration where required by the wider ecosystem.

== Experimental capabilities ==
AI editing, RAG, automated scoring, graph visualizers, unusual renderers, and other extensions may be tested without becoming part of the ontology.

== Specialized configurations ==
Different wiki instances or profiles may activate domain-specific capabilities such as:
* research and bibliography;
* biology and taxonomy;
* geospatial/environmental mapping;
* structured registries and datasets;
* knowledge-graph visualization;
* multilingual content;
* rich media and 3D;
* private memory and restricted namespaces;
* archival and publication workflows;
* AI laboratory tooling.

The scale of the MediaWiki extension ecosystem makes capability profiles more appropriate than one universal plugin stack.

== Rule ==
A plugin can enable behavior; it should not dictate what knowledge means.

